{"type": "FeatureCollection", "metadata": {"note": "The 'West Point Route': Atlanta to Montgomery, AL via West Point, GA, one of Montgomery's two historic rail approaches (see ln_nashville_birmingham_montgomery.geojson for the other, from Nashville via Birmingham). The Montgomery Railroad was chartered 1832, foreclosed and reorganized 1843 as the Montgomery and West Point Railroad, and completed Montgomery-West Point on April 28 1851 -- one of Alabama's earliest railroads. The separate Atlanta and West Point Rail Road completed the Atlanta-West Point link in 1854, giving through Atlanta-Montgomery service for the first time. On Sept 1 1870 the M&WP merged with the Western Rail Road to form the Western Railway of Alabama; that December, rails were extended west from Montgomery to Selma. So by 1900 Montgomery had had continuous through rail service to Atlanta for almost half a century. Montgomery's grand Union Station (built by the L&N, opened 1898) served this line's trains alongside Atlantic Coast Line, Seaboard Air Line, and Central of Georgia. Geometry is a real-town waypoint approximation (Atlanta-West Point-Opelika-Montgomery-Selma), straight-line segments between documented stations -- NOT digitized from period track maps.", "sources": ["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Montgomery_and_West_Point_Railroad (Montgomery Railroad chartered 1832, foreclosed 1842, reorganized as M&WP 1843, completed to West Point Apr 28 1851; Opelika-Columbus branch opened 1856)", "https://www.american-rails.com/point.html and https://www.trains.com/ctr/railroads/fallen-flags/west-point-route-history-remembered/ (Atlanta and West Point Rail Road completed 1854; M&WP/Western Rail Road merger Sept 1 1870 formed the Western Railway of Alabama; Montgomery-Selma extension Dec 1870)", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Montgomery_Union_Station (L&N-built station opened 1898; served ACL, Western Railway of Alabama, Seaboard Air Line, Central of Georgia, GM&O; rail service to the station ended 1979)", "https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=176411 (Atlanta's Zero Mile Post historical marker, 33 45.14'N 84 23.315'W = 33.75233,-84.38858; the original 1837 railroad terminus a block from Atlanta's historic Union Station, used to pin the Atlanta endpoint precisely rather than an approximate downtown coordinate)"], "not_yet_sourced": "Exact year through Atlanta-Montgomery passenger service on this route ended (modeled here as 1971, Amtrak's creation, since this route was not among those Amtrak selected to continue -- but it is possible some service continued slightly past or stopped somewhat before that under Seaboard Coast Line, unconfirmed); precise frequency for any era, all estimated; whether any named train specifically branded this route versus plain numbered locals.", "line_label_audit": "2026-08-24: line labels normalised repo-wide to \"<operating company>: <line name>\" -- the company's common display name (legal suffixes dropped), then the line as a passenger would have known it, or its two terminals where the line carried no advertised name. Long intermediate station lists were reduced to endpoints. For features spanning several operators the company shown is the one that ran the line for most of its drawn years. See CLAUDE.md Rule 6."}, "features": [{"type": "Feature", "properties": {"company": "Montgomery and West Point Railroad", "line": "Montgomery and West Point: Montgomery \u2013 West Point", "id": "mwp-montgomery-westpoint", "color": "#A663CC", "mode": "intercity_rail", "avg_speed_kmh": 30, "speed_source": "estimated: 1850s early steam-era railroad; unsourced", "segments": [{"start": 1851, "end": 1854, "headway_min": 720, "note": "Completed Montgomery-West Point April 28 1851, one of Alabama's earliest railroads, but not yet connected onward to Atlanta; frequency estimated/unsourced", "sources": ["NOT SOURCED AT SEGMENT LEVEL: the references below cover this line, but nothing has been checked specifically against this segment's start year, end year or servic